CARDS = [
# ---------- Node 38 ----------
("node-38", "Node 38 · /proc", "What is procfs, and what does it imply about every diagnostic tool?", "A filesystem with no storage: reading a file calls a kernel function that generates the content at that instant. So <code>ps</code>, <code>top</code>, <code>free</code>, <code>lsof</code>, node exporters, APM agents and container runtimes are all text parsers. There is no privileged metrics API, and <code>cat</code> is always enough."),
("node-38", "Node 38 · /proc", "Which per-process file does <code>top</code> read, and what is in it?", "<code>/proc/&lt;pid&gt;/stat</code> — a single machine-readable line of 52 fields including <code>utime</code> and <code>stime</code> in clock ticks, minor and major faults, and the start time."),
("node-38", "Node 38 · /proc", "How do you attribute CPU to one thread inside a Node process?", "<code>/proc/&lt;pid&gt;/task/</code> has one subdirectory per thread with the same files, which is what <code>top -H</code> reads."),
("node-38", "Node 38 · /proc", "What do <code>/proc/&lt;pid&gt;/stack</code> and <code>wchan</code> give you?", "The kernel-side stack and the function the process is sleeping in — so for a <code>D</code>-state hang they tell you exactly which kernel operation it is stuck in."),
("node-38", "Node 38 · /proc", "Why is every CPU percentage you have ever seen a derived number?", "<code>/proc/stat</code> holds cumulative counters since boot, not percentages. A tool samples it twice and divides the deltas — which is why a tool's first sample is meaningless and why average-since-boot is a different and usually useless number."),
("node-38", "Node 38 · /proc", "Name four system-wide files and what each answers.", "<code>/proc/meminfo</code> (what <code>free</code> formats — read <code>MemAvailable</code>); <code>/proc/loadavg</code> (which counts R <em>and</em> D tasks); <code>/proc/interrupts</code> (per-IRQ per-core counts, for interrupt balance); <code>/proc/net/tcp</code> (every socket with state and queue depths, what <code>ss</code> reads)."),
("node-38", "Node 38 · /proc", "What is <code>/proc/sys</code> and what is its relationship to <code>sysctl</code>?", "The writable half of procfs — every <code>sysctl</code> is a file there. <code>sysctl -w</code> is a file write; <code>/etc/sysctl.d/</code> makes it survive a reboot."),
("node-38", "Node 38 · /proc", "Where do container CPU, memory and throttling metrics actually come from?", "cgroup files under <code>/sys/fs/cgroup</code>: <code>memory.current</code>, <code>cpu.stat</code>'s <code>nr_throttled</code>. cAdvisor reads them; there is no other source behind your Grafana panel."),
("node-38", "Node 38 · /proc", "Why does the JVM need container support and Node need <code>availableParallelism()</code>?", "<code>/proc/cpuinfo</code> and <code>/proc/meminfo</code> are not namespaced and report the host's values, so any runtime sizing itself from them is wrong inside a container. The correct source is cgroup files; <code>lxcfs</code> exists to present a namespace-correct <code>/proc</code>."),
("node-38", "Node 38 · /proc", "Give the six-command sequence that distinguishes the five common production failures.", "<code>grep -E 'State|Threads|VmRSS|ctxt' /proc/$PID/status</code>; <code>ls /proc/$PID/fd | wc -l</code> against <code>grep 'Max open files' /proc/$PID/limits</code>; <code>cat /proc/$PID/stack</code> if in D state; <code>cat /sys/fs/cgroup/cpu.stat</code>; <code>grep -E '^(anon|file) ' /sys/fs/cgroup/memory.stat</code>. Between them: blocked loop, descriptor leak, storage hang, CPU throttling, page-cache memory pressure."),
("node-38", "Node 38 · /proc", "Which two custom metrics are most valuable for a containerised Node service?", "Event loop delay (from <code>monitorEventLoopDelay</code>) and cgroup <code>nr_throttled</code>, side by side. Node cannot tell you the second, so you read the file yourself."),
# ---------- Node 39 ----------
("node-39", "Node 39 · Tracing", "How does a debugger set a breakpoint?", "It writes <code>0xCC</code> (<code>int3</code>) over the first byte of the target instruction, saving the original. Execution raises an exception, delivered as <code>SIGTRAP</code>, suspending the process. To continue it restores the byte, single-steps, re-inserts, and resumes. A debugger edits your machine code while it runs — possible only because instructions are data."),
("node-39", "Node 39 · Tracing", "Why is <code>strace</code> unusable for production performance work?", "<code>ptrace</code> stops the traced process twice per system call, so a syscall-heavy program can slow by an order of magnitude. Excellent for correctness, wrong for performance."),
("node-39", "Node 39 · Tracing", "Why does <code>strace</code> on another process need <code>sudo</code>?", "Attaching means reading all its memory, including keys, so it is gated by <code>CAP_SYS_PTRACE</code> and by <code>ptrace_scope</code>, which by default permits attaching only to descendants. In containers this is <code>--cap-add=SYS_PTRACE</code>."),
("node-39", "Node 39 · Tracing", "How does <code>perf</code> avoid <code>strace</code>'s cost?", "Sampling. The processor's performance monitoring unit raises an interrupt every N events; <code>perf</code> records the instruction pointer and call stack at each, giving a statistical picture at one to two percent overhead."),
("node-39", "Node 39 · Tracing", "What can <code>perf stat</code> tell you that a wall-clock measurement cannot?", "Why it is slow. Hardware counters give instructions retired, cache misses per level, branch mispredictions and TLB misses — so a low instructions-per-cycle figure with high cache misses is a memory-layout problem, not an algorithmic one."),
("node-39", "Node 39 · Tracing", "How do you read a flame graph correctly?", "Width is the proportion of samples in which a function was on the stack; the vertical axis is stack depth; the left-to-right ordering is alphabetical and means nothing. A wide plateau at the top is a function burning CPU in itself; a wide tower is a deep call path."),
("node-39", "Node 39 · Tracing", "How is eBPF reconciled with the rule that user code must never run privileged?", "A verifier statically proves the bytecode terminates, that every memory access is in bounds, and that it calls only permitted helpers, before JIT-compiling it. A proof obligation substituting for a hardware boundary."),
("node-39", "Node 39 · Tracing", "What does eBPF change about the economics of observability?", "Aggregation happens <em>in the kernel</em> — a program can compute a latency histogram of every disk I/O and hand you the finished histogram, instead of copying every event to user space. That is what makes it acceptable in production."),
("node-39", "Node 39 · Tracing", "Name four eBPF tools and what each answers.", "<code>execsnoop</code> — every process executed, as it happens. <code>biolatency</code> — a histogram of block-device latency. <code>runqlat</code> — how long tasks wait in the run queue, which is the CPU-contention signal utilisation cannot show and where throttling appears. <code>tcplife</code> — connection lifetimes."),
("node-39", "Node 39 · Tracing", "Name the three layers of Node profiling and what each sees.", "JavaScript-level (<code>--prof</code>, DevTools, <code>0x</code>) — your function names, via V8's sampling profiler. Native-level (<code>perf</code> plus <code>--perf-basic-prof</code> so JIT frames resolve) — V8, libuv, addons and the kernel, which the JavaScript profiler cannot see at all. Kernel-level (eBPF) — what it is waiting for."),
("node-39", "Node 39 · Tracing", "Give the diagnostic sequence for a slow Node service.", "High event loop delay and high CPU → the JavaScript profiler. High CPU with no JavaScript function accounting for it → <code>perf</code>; the answer is usually GC, JSON, TLS or an addon. Low CPU with high latency → the process is waiting, and eBPF tools say for what."),
("node-39", "Node 39 · Tracing", "Why is a buffer leak invisible in a heap snapshot, and how do you find it?", "Those bytes are outside V8's heap. It shows as rising RSS with a flat heap; find it with <code>perf</code> or by auditing addon usage."),
("node-39", "Node 39 · Tracing", "Why is continuous production profiling now normal?", "Because sampling costs one to two percent, running it permanently across a fleet is affordable — and it changes the economics: instead of reproducing a problem, you look at what the process was doing when it happened."),
# ---------- Node 40 ----------
("node-40", "Node 40 · The boundary", "What is Meltdown, in one sentence, and why did the fix cost so much?", "Speculative execution let user-mode code read kernel memory — mapped into every process for efficiency — by inferring values through cache timing. KPTI unmaps kernel memory from the user page table, so every syscall now also switches the page table root and disturbs address translation."),
("node-40", "Node 40 · The boundary", "Name three other speculative-execution mitigations and what each costs.", "Retpoline/IBRS/IBPB constrain branch prediction across privilege boundaries, adding cost to indirect calls and context switches; MDS and L1TF mitigations flush buffers on transitions; and on some hardware disabling hyperthreading was the only complete answer, halving the apparent core count."),
("node-40", "Node 40 · The boundary", "When is <code>mitigations=off</code> defensible?", "On a single-tenant machine running only trusted code. Indefensible on anything running untrusted code, which includes any machine running a browser or a multi-tenant workload. <code>/sys/devices/system/cpu/vulnerabilities/*</code> lists what is being mitigated."),
("node-40", "Node 40 · The boundary", "Name the three strategies for crossing the boundary less, with an instance of each.", "Do not cross (the vDSO answers <code>clock_gettime</code> in user mode at ~20 ns); batch (<code>writev</code>, <code>sendfile</code>, buffered output, epoll itself); replace the interface (<code>io_uring</code>)."),
("node-40", "Node 40 · The boundary", "What is <code>sendfile</code> and why is nginx cheaper than an app serving the same file?", "It copies from one descriptor to another entirely inside the kernel — no copy into user memory and back, which is the actual meaning of zero-copy. The application path copies the bytes twice for no reason."),
("node-40", "Node 40 · The boundary", "Describe io_uring's structure and its four properties.", "Two ring buffers shared between the process and the kernel — a submission queue and a completion queue. Many operations per crossing; optionally zero crossings in <code>SQPOLL</code> mode; completion-based rather than readiness-based, which is what makes it work for regular files; and it covers nearly every operation, with chaining."),
("node-40", "Node 40 · The boundary", "Why is io_uring blocked by some seccomp profiles and disabled in ChromeOS?", "Its rapid growth in surface area produced a run of serious security vulnerabilities. Blocking it is done by filtering its syscall numbers — a nice illustration of Node 4."),
("node-40", "Node 40 · The boundary", "Quantify why syscall count is a design axis in a request handler.", "2,000 syscalls at ~100 ns each is 200 µs of pure crossing — ten percent of a 2 ms budget. Hence keeping a log descriptor open, writing a response once rather than forty times, HTTP keep-alive, a Unix socket instead of loopback, and <code>stream.pipeline</code>. <code>strace -c -f</code> gives you the count, and almost nobody takes that measurement."),
("node-40", "Node 40 · The boundary", "Show that the N+1 query problem is the same optimisation as <code>writev</code>.", "A syscall crossing is ~100 ns; a local database round trip ~200 µs — two thousand times more. 100 queries is 20 ms of pure round-trip time; one query returning the same rows is 200 µs. DataLoader batching, <code>IN (...)</code>, Redis pipelining and HTTP/2 multiplexing are all the same move at a larger scale."),
("node-40", "Node 40 · The boundary", "State the general principle this node establishes.", "Every layer has a boundary, and every layer's optimisation is to cross it less often with more in hand. Batch, or move the work to the other side. Those are the only two moves at every scale."),
]
